== Chapter V

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The nature of sin further laid open as it is enmity against God. Its aversion from all good explained. Means prescribed to prevent its effects.]
]

#smallcaps[WE] have considered something of the nature of #emph[indwelling sin], not #emph[absolutely], but with a view to discovering its power. But its power shows itself more clearly in its #emph[actions] and #emph[workings]. Power is an act of life, and working is the only thing that reveals life. We do not know that anything lives except by the effects and works of life. And great and strong workings reveal a powerful and vigorous life. Such are the workings of this #emph[law of sin], and they are all demonstrations of its power.

What we have declared about its nature is that it consists in #emph[enmity]. Now there are two general heads of the working or operation of #emph[enmity]: first, #emph[aversion]\; secondly, #emph[opposition].

First, #emph[aversion]. Our Savior, describing by its effects the #emph[enmity] that was between himself and the teachers of the Jews, says in the prophet, “My soul loathed them, and their souls also detested me” (Zech 11:8). Where there is mutual enmity, there is mutual #emph[aversion], loathing, and abhorrence. So it was between the Jews and the Samaritans: they were enemies and abhorred one another, as in John 4:9.

Secondly, #emph[opposition], or contending against one another, is the next product of #emph[enmity]. Isa 63:10: “So He turned and became their enemy, and He Himself fought against them,” speaking of God toward the people. Where there is #emph[enmity], there will be #emph[fighting]\; it is its proper and natural product. Now both these #emph[effects] are found in this #emph[law of sin].

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, as for #emph[aversion], there is an aversion in it to God and to everything of God, as we have partly shown in handling the #emph[enmity] itself, so we will not need to dwell on it much again. All #emph[unfitness] for #emph[duty] in which communion with God is to be obtained, all #emph[weariness] of duty, all carnality or #emph[formality] in duty, springs from this root. The wise man warns us against this evil in Eccl 5:1: “Guard your steps when you go to the house of God.” Do you have some spiritual duty to perform, and do you aim at attaining some communion with God? Look to yourself; take care of your #emph[affections]. They will be roaming and wandering, and that from their #emph[aversion] to what you have in hand. There is no good that we want to do in which we may not find this #emph[aversion] at work. “When I want to do good, evil is present with me”: at any time, at all times, when I want to do anything spiritually good, it is present. That is, it is present to #emph[hinder me], to obstruct me in my duty; because it abhors and loathes the thing I have in hand, it will keep me from it if it possibly can. In those in whom it prevails, it comes at length to the frame described in Ezek 33:31. It will allow an outward #emph[bodily presence] at the worship of God, in which it has no concern, but it keeps the heart entirely away.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Perhaps some will claim that they do not find it so in #emph[themselves], but that they have #emph[freedom] and #emph[liberty] in and toward all the duties of obedience they attend to. But I fear that on examination this #emph[claimed liberty] will be found to arise from one or both of these causes. First, #emph[ignorance] of the true state and condition of their own souls, of their inner man and its actions toward God. They do not know how it is with them, and so they are not to be believed in what they report. They are in the #emph[dark], and know neither what they are doing nor where they are going. It is likely that the #emph[Pharisee] knew little of this matter, which made him #emph[boast] of his duties to God himself. Or, secondly, perhaps whatever duties of worship or obedience such persons perform, they may have no communion with God in them, through lack of faith and of a share in Christ. And if so, #emph[sin] will make only a little opposition to them in those duties. We are speaking of #emph[those] whose hearts are exercised with these things. And if, while they complain of them and groan for deliverance from them, others cry out to them, “Keep to yourselves; … we are holier than you#super[+],” they are willing to bear their condition. They know that their way may be safe, though it is troublesome, and they are willing to see their own dangers, so that they may avoid the ruin others fall into.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Let us, then, consider this #emph[aversion] a little in those acts of obedience in which nothing is concerned but God and the soul. In public duties there may be a mixture of other considerations. They may be so influenced by custom and necessity that a right judgment of this matter cannot be made from them. But let us take into consideration the duties of #emph[retirement], such as #emph[private prayer] and #emph[meditation] and the like, or else extraordinary duties, or duties to be performed in an extraordinary manner.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, in these this #emph[aversion] and loathing will often show itself in the #emph[affections]. There will be a secret struggle in them about close and heartfelt dealing with God. Convictions, the sense of duty, a dear and real esteem for God and for communion with him, may have carried the soul into its private room. Yet unless the hand of God by his Spirit is high and strong on the soul, if the vigor and power of a #emph[spiritual life] is not constantly at work, there will be a secret reluctance in the affections toward duty. Indeed, at times there will be a violent inclination to the contrary, so that the soul would rather do anything, would welcome any #emph[distraction], though it wounds itself by it, than vigorously apply itself to what it longs for in the inner man. It is #emph[weary] before it begins, and says, “When will the work be over?” Here God and the soul are immediately concerned, and it is a great conquest to #emph[do what we want to do], though we come far short of what #emph[we ought] to do. Secondly, it shows itself in the #emph[mind] also. When we come to God in Christ, we are, as #emph[Job] speaks, “to fill our mouths with arguments” (Job 23:4), so that we may be able to #emph[plead] with him, as he calls on us to do (Isa 43:26): “Remind Me, let us argue the matter together.” That is why the church is called on to take #emph[words] or arguments with it in going to God (Hos 14:2). The sum is that the #emph[mind] should be furnished with the considerations that prevail with God, and be ready to #emph[plead] them, and to manage them in the most spiritual manner to the best advantage. Now is there no difficulty in getting the #emph[mind] into such a frame that it spends itself to the utmost in this work? That it is clear, steady, and constant in its duty? That it draws out and makes use of its stores and supply of promises and experiences? It #emph[starts aside], wanders, #emph[flags], all from this secret #emph[aversion] to communion with God, which comes from the #emph[law of indwelling sin]. Some complain that they can make nothing of #emph[meditation]\; they cannot bend their minds to it. I admit that a great cause of this may be their lack of a right understanding of the duty itself and of the ways of managing the soul in it, and so I will speak to that a little later. But still this #emph[secret enmity] also has its hand in the difficulty they are at, both in their minds and in their affections. Others find so little benefit and success in #emph[private] duties that they are forced to live on #emph[family] and #emph[public] duties. And here has been the beginning of the #emph[apostasy] of many professors, and the source of many foolish, sensual opinions. They found this #emph[aversion] in their minds and affections from closeness and constancy in private spiritual duties. Not knowing how to conquer and prevail against these difficulties through “him who gives us strength,” they were at first overcome so far as to neglect them, first #emph[partly], then #emph[wholly]. At last, having lost all conscience about them, they had a door opened to all sin and licentiousness, and so to a full and utter #emph[apostasy]. I am persuaded that there are very few who #emph[fall away] from a profession of any long standing (and our days abound with such) whose door of entrance into the folly of backsliding was not one of two things. Either it was some #emph[great and notorious sin] that bloodied their consciences, tainted their affections, and cut off all delight in having anything more to do with God; or else it was a course of #emph[neglect of private duties], arising from weariness of contending against that powerful #emph[aversion] to them that they found in themselves. And through the craft of Satan this too has been turned into many foolish and sensual opinions about #emph[living to God without, and above, any duties of communion]. And we find that after men have choked and blinded their consciences with this pretense for a while, cursed wickedness or sensuality has been the end of their folly. And the reason for all this is that to give way to the #emph[law of sin] in the least is to give it strength. To let it alone is to let it #emph[grow]\; not to conquer it is to be conquered by it.]

As it is with #emph[private] duties, so it is also with #emph[public] duties that have anything #emph[extraordinary] in them. What strivings, struggles, and pleadings there are in the heart about them, especially against their spirituality! Indeed, in and during them, will not the mind and affections at times be entangled with things #emph[odd], new and strange to them, things a man would not stoop to take into his thoughts at a time of the least serious business? But if the least looseness, liberty or advantage is given to #emph[indwelling sin], if it is not perpetually watched, it will work toward a strange and unexpected outcome. In brief, let the soul strip any duty whatever, #emph[private] or #emph[public], anything that is called #emph[good], of all the outward considerations that secretly work their way into the mind. These give the mind some #emph[satisfaction] in what it is doing, but do not make the duty acceptable to God. Let a man take them away, and he will surely find something of the power and some of the effects of this aversion. It begins in reluctance and unfitness, goes on to entangle the mind and affections with other things, and, if it is not prevented, will end in #emph[weariness] of God, which he complains of in his people (Isa 43:22). They gave up duty because they were weary of God.

But this instance is of great importance to professors in their walk with God, so we must not pass it over without some hints of direction for them in contending against it and opposing it. Only this must be said first. I am not giving directions for mortifying #emph[indwelling sin] in general, which is to be done by the Spirit of Christ alone, by virtue of our union with him (Rom 8:13). I am giving directions only for our #emph[particular duty] with respect to this special evil or effect of #emph[indwelling sin] that we have dwelt on a little, or what the wisdom of faith seems to direct to and call for in this #emph[single case]. And this will be our way and course as we go on to consider its other effects.

First, the great means of preventing the fruits and effects of this #emph[aversion] is to keep the soul constantly in a universally holy frame. As this weakens the whole #emph[law of sin], so it weakens all its properties correspondingly, and this #emph[aversion] in particular. It is this #emph[frame] alone that will enable us to say with the #emph[psalmist] (Ps 57:7), “My heart is steadfast, O God, my heart is steadfast.” It is utterly impossible to keep the heart in a prevailing holy frame in #emph[any one duty] unless it is so in and toward #emph[all and every one]. If the entanglements of sin get hold in #emph[one thing], they will force themselves on the soul in #emph[everything]. A constant, even frame and temper in all duties, in #emph[all ways], is the only safeguard for any #emph[one] way. Let no man who is negligent in #emph[public] duties persuade himself that all will be clear and easy in #emph[private], or the other way round. There is a #emph[harmony] in obedience: break only one part and you interrupt the whole. Our particular wounds generally arise from negligence in the whole course. So #emph[David] tells us in Ps 119:6: “Then I would not be ashamed when I consider all Your commandments.” A universal regard for all God’s commandments is the only safeguard from #emph[shame]. And there is nothing we have more reason to be ashamed of than the shameful failures of our hearts in matters of duty, which come from the principle mentioned before.

Secondly, labor to prevent the very #emph[beginnings] of the workings of this aversion. Let grace be beforehand with it in every duty. We are directed in 1 Pet 4:7 to “be clear-minded and watchful, so that you#super[+] can pray”; and as it is with prayer, so with every duty. That is, we are to consider and take care that we are not hindered, from within or from without, in performing it as we should. #emph[Watch] against temptations, to oppose them; watch against the aversion that is in sin, to prevent it. As we are not to “give the devil a foothold,” no more are we to give one to sin. If it is not prevented in its first attempts, it will prevail. My meaning is this. Whatever #emph[good], as the apostle speaks, we have to do, and “find evil present with us” (and we will find it present), we must prevent its parleying with the soul, its working its poison into the mind and affections. We must do it by a vigorous, holy, violent stirring up of the grace or graces that are to be exercised and set to work particularly in that duty. Let #emph[Jacob] come into the world first; or if he is forestalled by the violence of #emph[Esau], let him take hold of his heel to overthrow him and obtain the birthright. At #emph[Peter]’s very first suggestion to our Savior, when he cried, “Far be it from You, Lord!” our Savior immediately replied, “Get behind Me, Satan!” So we ought to say, “Be gone, you #emph[law of sin], you present evil,” and it may be of the same use to us. Get grace up early to duty, then, and be early in rebuking sin.

Thirdly, though it does its worst, make sure that it never prevails to a #emph[conquest]. Make sure that you are not #emph[worn] out by its persistence, nor driven from your hold by its pestering. Do not lose heart because of its opposition. Take the apostle’s advice in Heb 6:11: “We want each of you#super[+] to show this same diligence to the very end, in order to make your#super[+] hope sure. Then you#super[+] will not be sluggish.” Hold out still in the same diligence. There are many ways in which men are driven from a constant, holy performance of duties, and all of them are dangerous, if not deadly, to the soul. Some are drawn away by #emph[business], some by company, some by the power of temptations; some are discouraged by their own darkness. But none is so #emph[dangerous] as this: when the soul gives up, in #emph[part] or in #emph[whole], worn out by the aversion of sin to the duty, or to #emph[communion] with God in it. This shows that the soul is giving itself up to the power of sin, which will surely prove ruinous unless the Lord breaks the snare of Satan in it. Our Savior’s instruction is that we ought “to pray at all times and not lose heart” (Luke 18:1). Opposition will arise, and none so bitter and sharp as that from our own hearts. If we lose heart, we perish. “Take care,” says the apostle, “so that you#super[+] will not grow weary and lose heart” (Heb 12:3). Such a #emph[losing of heart] as goes with a #emph[weariness], and with giving way to the aversion working in our hearts, is to be avoided if we would not perish. The caution is the same as that of the same apostle in Rom 12:12: “Be joyful in hope, patient in affliction, persistent in prayer.” And in general it is the same as Rom 6:12: “Therefore do not let sin reign in your#super[+] mortal body so that you#super[+] obey its lusts.” To give up duty, in part or in whole, because of sin’s aversion to its spirituality, is to give sin the rule and to obey it in its lusts. Do not yield to it, then, but hold out in the conflict. Wait on God and you#super[+] will prevail (Isa 40:31): “But those who wait upon the #smallcaps[Lord] will renew their strength; they will mount up with wings like eagles; they will run and not grow weary, they will walk and not faint.” What is now so difficult will grow more difficult if we give way to it. But if we stay at our post, we will prevail; the mouth of the Lord has spoken it.

Fourthly, carry about with you a constant #emph[humbling sense] of this hidden #emph[aversion] to spirituality that still lies in our nature. If men find its efficacy, what consideration #emph[should], what consideration #emph[can], be more powerful to bring them to walk humbly with God? After all the discoveries God has made of himself to them, all the kindness they have received from him, his doing them good and not evil in all things, there should still remain such a heart of unkindness and unbelief as to have an #emph[aversion] lying in it to communion with him! How the thought of it ought to cast us into the dust and fill us with shame and self-abhorrence all our days! What have we found in God in any of our approaches or addresses to him, that it should be this way with us? What iniquity have we found in him? “Has he been a wilderness to us, or a land of dense darkness?” Did we ever lose anything by drawing near to him? No, has not all the rest and peace we have obtained lain in this? Is he not the fountain and spring of all our mercies, of all our desirable things? Has he not made us welcome when we came? Have we not received from him more than the heart can conceive or the tongue express? What is wrong, then, with our foolish and wretched hearts, that they harbor such a cursed secret dislike of him and his ways? Let us be ashamed and astonished when we consider it, and walk in a humbling sense of it all our days. Let us carry it about with us in the most secret of our thoughts. And as this is a #emph[duty] acceptable to God in itself, who delights to dwell with those who are of a “humble and contrite spirit,” so it has exceeding efficacy in weakening the evil we are treating.

Fifthly, labor to fill the mind with the beauty and excellence of spiritual things, so that they may be presented to the soul as #emph[lovely] and desirable; and this cursed #emph[aversion] of sin will be weakened by it. It is an inborn, acknowledged principle that the soul of man will not keep on cheerfully in the #emph[worship of God] unless it discovers a #emph[beauty] and attractiveness in it. That is why, when men had lost all spiritual sense and relish of the things of God, they invented outwardly pompous and gorgeous ways of worship, to supply the lack that was in their own souls: #emph[images, paintings, pictures], and who knows what carnal ornaments, which they have called the “beauties of holiness.” This much, however, was revealed in it: the mind of man must see a #emph[beauty], a desirableness, in the things of God’s worship, or it will not delight in it, and #emph[aversion] will prevail. Let the soul, then, labor to become acquainted with the #emph[spiritual beauty] of obedience, of communion with God, and of all duties of immediate approach to him, so that it may be filled with #emph[delight] in them. It is not my present work to lay open the heads and springs of the #emph[beauty] and desirableness that is in spiritual duties: in their relation to #emph[God], the eternal spring of all beauty; to #emph[Christ], the love, desire, and hope of all nations; to the #emph[Spirit], the great beautifier of souls, who by his grace makes them all glorious within; in their fitness to the souls of men as to their actions toward their last end; and in the rightness and holiness of the #emph[rule] by which they are to be performed. I only say at present, in general, that to acquaint the soul thoroughly with these things is an eminent way of weakening the #emph[aversion] spoken of.
